===persian===

به شوقِ روییدن
به دامِ آب افتادم...
و رود... رویای دوری بود....
و برکه... حسرتِ بی‌راهی...
نسیم دریا به خوابِ من می‌آمد....
و طعمِ لب‌هایم را شورتر می‌کرد....
به شوق روییدن...
به تاب افتادم... به دامِ آب افتادم...
و ابر... حسرتِ خورشیدِ تشنه...
و ابرِ... روحِ آبِ گریزان از آفتاب...
باران... خموش و غمناک...
همیشه در برگشت به ابتدای خاک...
در آرزوی عبور از هزار ساقهٔ تاک...
و داربستِ مو چه ساقه‌هایی داشت....
درخواب‌های کودکیم...
زیرِ سایهٔ نمناک، حبابِ ماه را بغل می‌کردم...
۱۳۶۹

===machine===

For the longing to sprout
I fell into the trap of water...
and the river... was a distant dream....
and the pool... the regret of having no road...
The sea breeze would come into my sleep....
and make the taste of my lips saltier....
For the longing to sprout...
I fell to swaying... I fell into the trap of water...
and the cloud... the regret of a thirsty sun...
and the cloud... the soul of water in flight from the sunlight...
Rain... silent and sorrowful...
forever returning to the beginning of earth...
longing to pass through a thousand vine-stems...
and what stems the grape trellis had....
In the dreams of my childhood...
under the damp shade, I held the bubble of the moon in my arms...
1369

===lantern===

Out of an ardour to grow
I fell into the water's trap...
and the river... was a far-off dream....
and the pool... the regret of having no road...
The sea breeze would come into my sleep....
and leave the taste of my lips saltier....
Out of an ardour to grow...
I fell to swaying... I fell into the water's trap...
and the cloud... the regret of a thirsty sun...
and the cloud... the soul of water fleeing the sunlight...
Rain... silent and sorrowful...
forever going back to the beginning of clay...
longing to pass through a thousand vine-stems...
and what stems that grape trellis had....
In the dreams of my childhood,
under the damp shade, I would take the bubble of the moon in my arms...

1369

===translation===

===footnotes===

روییدن, to come up as a plant does, is the verb the book keeps returning to — a
shadow grows behind the curtain of nothing in Jafari's opening page, a peasant's fortune has grown
like a shadow beside his feet in Farrokhi's introduction — and here the ardour to grow is exactly
what lands him in the water. دام, the trap, is the word drunk-waterfall uses for the snare of its
own white snows, thirty years earlier: the same figure of water held where it does not want to
be.

